{
  "dom_id": "step-t2000",
  "name": "Introduction",
  "problem": "**Volume** is the amount of space that an object takes up. We can measure volume with **unit cubes**, like the one below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14132963/assets/a318f6234bdbf014e4cbefbc1add274dbcc37b08f59d93a5fe53c463e962cc86.png)\n\nThese are called unit cubes because each one represents $1$ cubic unit. The volume of a figure is the number of unit cubes that make it up.\n\nFor example, let's find the volume of the figure below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14132963/assets/1215ed078257410d4aab5cff0a58dcbc719c477211bdec8a77b9d10a2ecb5dae.png)\n\nThe figure consists of $2$ cubes. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14132963/assets/4ba5e58d16c4bf9e684590e0280c4ddbd32c5b2c2bbf94412ca77713d9f059b0.png)\n\nTherefore, its volume is $2$ cubic units.",
